Democratic senator [[Donald W. Riegle Jr.]] retired after three terms. Former [[Michigan Republican Party]] Chairman [[Spencer Abraham]] defeated Democratic Congressman [[Milton Robert Carr]] in the race to succeed Riegle.

Riegle, a three-term incumbent, was considered one of the most vulnerable Senate Democrats in the 1994 mid-term elections due to the unpopularity of [[Bill Clinton|President Bill Clinton]]<ref>{{Cite news |last=Richard L. Berke |date=July 27, 1993 |title=Senate Democrats See Re-election Perils in '94 |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1993/07/27/us/senate-democrats-see-re-election-perils-in-94.html?pagewanted=all |access-date=October 17, 2017}}</ref> and his being involved as a member of the [[Keating Five]], a group of five senators who were accused of corruption. After months of speculation, Riegle announced he would not seek a 4th term in a speech on the Senate floor.<ref>{{Cite web |last=William J. Eaton |date=September 29, 1993 |title=Riegle Is 3rd Keating Case Senator to Not Seek Office |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1993-09-29-mn-40330-story.html |access-date=October 17, 2017 |website=Los Angeles Times}}</ref>

