{{Main|1994 United States Senate election in New York}}
{{See also|List of United States senators from New York}}
Veteran Democratic incumbent [[Daniel Patrick Moynihan]] easily defeated his Republican opponent, businesswoman [[Bernadette Castro]].

1994 was significant for the [[Republican Revolution]], mostly as a referendum against President [[Bill Clinton]] and [[Hillarycare|his health care plan]], and was seen as a tough year for Democratic incumbents. Moynihan, however, was New York State's most popular politician at the time, and ran ahead of all other Democrats competing statewide.<ref name="nyt110994">{{Cite news |last=Finder, Alan |date=November 9, 1994 |title=Defying Anti-Incumbent Mood, Moynihan Wins Easily |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1994/11/09/nyregion/1994-elections-new-york-state-senate-defying-anti-incumbent-mood-moynihan-wins.html}}</ref>

Republican Castro was running for office for the first time and had trouble raising funds due to being seen as unlikely to win; at times during the race she trailed by up to 30 percentage points.<ref name="nyt110994" /> She portrayed herself as a fiscally conservative, socially moderate Republican in the mold of Governor of New Jersey [[Christie Todd Whitman]], and attempted to portray Moynihan as excessively liberal and prone to government spending.<ref name="nyt110994" /> But Moynihan repeated his past strong performance among upstate voters, in addition to the usual Democratic strongholds in New York City.<ref name="nyt110994" />

